            "A <strong>communicable disease</strong> is an illness caused by an infectious agent — a bacterium, virus, fungus, protozoan or helminth — or by its toxins, which can be passed from an infected person, animal or reservoir to a susceptible host, directly or through an intermediate. Ghana has made real progress: Guinea worm transmission was certified interrupted in 2015, trachoma was validated as eliminated in 2018, neonatal tetanus elimination was validated in 2011, and wild poliovirus has not been seen for two decades. Yet malaria is still the commonest reason a child attends an outpatient department, tuberculosis notifications rose to about 19,000 in 2023 and the country still finds only a part of its true cases, about 334,000 people live with HIV, cholera returned in October 2024 and spread through five southern regions, the meningitis belt districts of the north count cases every dry season, and in a single three-year span the country met Marburg for the first time (Ashanti, 2022), a Lassa fever cluster in Accra (2023), human anthrax in the Upper East (2023), its first major dengue outbreak (Eastern and Central Regions, 2024) and hundreds of cases of mpox (2025).",
            "Earlier courses in this series have already taught the pieces: the <em>Infection Prevention & Control</em> course covered hand hygiene, standard and transmission-based precautions, decontamination and the IPC programme; the <em>Community & Public Health Nursing</em> course covered IDSR, the control programmes and water and sanitation; the system courses covered tuberculosis under respiratory nursing, cholera and typhoid under gastrointestinal nursing and HIV under haematology and immunology; and the <em>Paediatric Nursing & Immunisation</em> course covered the vaccines and IMCI. This course puts the diseases themselves at the centre. For each one you will learn the agent and the reservoir, how it travels, how it presents, how it is confirmed, how it is treated and nursed in a Ghanaian facility, how the patient is isolated and the contacts protected, how it is reported, and how it is prevented in the community — because that is the sequence the licensing examination follows and the sequence the ward demands.",
            "Why does a middle-income country with a health insurance scheme still carry this burden? Because the determinants of infection sit mostly outside the hospital: a fifth of households still lack a basic latrine and open defecation persists in many districts; piped water reaches a minority of rural homes and stored water is recontaminated; the climate suits the Anopheles and Aedes mosquito, the blackfly and the freshwater snail; crowding in markets, lorry parks, boarding schools, prisons and mining camps suits tuberculosis and meningococcus; cross-border movement and funerals carry disease between communities; and poverty delays presentation until the patient is already severely ill — most of the deaths in the 2024–25 cholera outbreak occurred within twenty-four hours of admission after days of self-medication at home. Ghana therefore carries a <strong>double burden</strong>: the communicable diseases of poverty alongside the hypertension, diabetes and cancer of a changing lifestyle, often in the same patient.",
