            "The susceptible host is the link the vaccinator attacks. <strong>Innate</strong> immunity is the non-specific defence everyone is born with — intact skin and mucosa, stomach acid, cilia, the cough, fever, complement, phagocytes and the normal flora that crowd out invaders, which is why broad-spectrum antibiotics are followed by thrush and <em>Clostridioides difficile</em> diarrhoea. <strong>Acquired</strong> immunity is specific and is sorted by two questions. Who made the antibody? If the patient's own body did, after infection or vaccination, it is <strong>active</strong>: slow to develop over days to weeks, long-lasting and backed by memory cells. If the antibody was made elsewhere and handed over — across the placenta and in breast milk, or as tetanus or rabies immunoglobulin, or as the antivenom that is really a passive antibody — it is <strong>passive</strong>: immediate but fading over weeks to months, with no memory. Was it acquired naturally or given deliberately? Having measles is natural active immunity; the measles vaccine is artificial active; maternal antibody is natural passive; immunoglobulin is artificial passive. The deep contaminated wound in an unimmunised farmer needs both — immunoglobulin for protection now and toxoid for protection later — and the two are given at different sites.",
            "<strong>Herd immunity</strong> (herd protection) is the indirect protection of the unvaccinated when enough of the community is immune that the chain of transmission cannot be sustained. The threshold depends on how infectious the disease is — about 95 per cent for measles, which is why a district reporting 85 per cent coverage can still have a measles outbreak, and why coverage must be high in every sub-district and settlement rather than high on average. Herd immunity is what protects the infant too young for the measles–rubella vaccine, the child on chemotherapy and the person who genuinely cannot be vaccinated; it does not work for tetanus, which comes from soil rather than from other people, so every individual must be immunised against it.",
