            "A count of cases tells you almost nothing until you know how many people it came from. <strong>Incidence</strong> is the number of <em>new</em> cases arising in a defined population over a defined period — 40 new tuberculosis cases in a district of 100,000 in a year is an incidence of 40 per 100,000 per year — and it measures the speed at which a disease is spreading, so it is the number that answers ‘is the problem getting worse?’ <strong>Prevalence</strong> is the number of <em>all</em> existing cases, old and new, at one moment (point prevalence) or over a period, divided by the population — and it measures the burden the service must carry: beds, drugs, clinic slots. A long illness has high prevalence and low incidence; a short illness like cholera has high incidence and low prevalence. The classic examination trap is HIV: antiretroviral therapy keeps people alive for decades, so the prevalence rises while new infections fall. Rising prevalence here is good news, not failure.",
