            "Fever in Ghana is not automatically malaria, and the history is the cheapest investigation. Ask about <strong>duration and pattern</strong>: hours to days suggests malaria, a viral illness, pneumonia or meningitis; a week or more suggests typhoid, hepatitis, HIV seroconversion, brucellosis, an abscess, tuberculosis or endocarditis. Ask what else the patient has — the <strong>associated symptoms</strong> that give the fever its company (below). Ask about <strong>travel and residence</strong> in the last month: the forest zone and the dry north differ, a visit to a village where ‘the rats are many’ raises Lassa, a stay in a crowded northern compound in March raises meningitis, a trip across the border or a funeral raises cholera, Ebola and Marburg. Ask about <strong>contacts</strong> — anyone at home or work with a similar illness, a rash, a cough for weeks, a death. Ask about <strong>animals</strong> — a dog bite or scratch, handling or eating a dead animal, unpasteurised milk, bats in a cave or mine, rodents in the food store. Ask about <strong>water and food</strong> — the source of drinking water, swimming or washing in the river or the lake, street food, unwashed fruit. Ask about the <strong>season</strong>: meningitis and measles peak in the dry harmattan months, malaria and cholera after the rains. Ask about <strong>immunisation</strong> status in a child and tetanus and yellow fever in an adult, <strong>HIV status</strong> and the date of the last test, <strong>pregnancy</strong>, <strong>medicines</strong> taken in the last two weeks including anti-malarials bought at the chemical seller, antibiotics, herbal preparations and anything injected, and <strong>occupation</strong> — farmer, herdsman, butcher, miner, health worker, fisherman, sex worker, long-distance driver.",
