            "<strong>Sepsis</strong> is life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection; <strong>septic shock</strong> is sepsis with hypotension needing vasopressors and a raised lactate despite fluid. On the ward, suspect it when a patient with a known or suspected infection develops any of: new confusion or drowsiness, a respiratory rate of 22 or more, a systolic blood pressure of 100 mmHg or less, a heart rate above 130, saturation below 92 per cent, urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/hour, mottled or cold peripheries, a non-blanching rash, or a lactate above 2 mmol/L — or an early warning score that is rising. The temperature may be high, normal or low; hypothermia in sepsis is a worse sign than fever, and the malnourished child, the neutropenic patient, the older person and the patient on steroids may be septic without a fever at all. Every hour of delay in antibiotics increases mortality.",
            "The <strong>Sepsis Six</strong>, completed within one hour of recognition, is three things taken out and three put in. Out: blood cultures (and the malaria test, the lactate if available, and a full blood count, urea and electrolytes); the lactate; and a urine output measurement — catheterise and chart hourly. In: high-flow oxygen to keep the saturation at 94 to 98 per cent (88 to 92 in chronic lung disease); intravenous fluid — a 500 mL crystalloid bolus repeated to 30 mL/kg in adults, 10 to 20 mL/kg boluses in children with reassessment after each because the malnourished and anaemic child drowns easily; and broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics according to the facility's guideline and the likely source, within the hour and without waiting for the culture result, narrowed when the sensitivity returns. Call for senior help early; a patient who remains hypotensive after fluid needs a vasopressor and a higher level of care. And in Ghana the Sepsis Six has a seventh line: <strong>could this be severe malaria?</strong> Fever with confusion has more than one cause here, and a septic-looking patient is given artesunate and antibiotics together until the film is read.",
            "In the febrile <strong>child</strong>, IMCI's general danger signs decide the pace: unable to drink or breastfeed, vomits everything, convulsions, lethargic or unconscious — any one means urgent referral and pre-referral treatment. Add the stiff neck, the bulging fontanelle, the petechial rash, fast breathing by age, chest indrawing, severe pallor, the sunken eyes with a slow skin pinch, a temperature of 38 °C in an infant under two months (who goes to hospital whatever the test says), a fever in a child with sickle cell disease (who has intravenous antibiotics after cultures because her spleen does not work) and the fever of the child on chemotherapy. The <strong>nursing of fever itself</strong> is simple: paracetamol 15 mg/kg (adult 1 g) four- to six-hourly, not more than four doses in twenty-four hours, with ibuprofen as a second line in a well-hydrated child and never aspirin in a child because of Reye syndrome; light clothing and a fan-cooled room, not a cold bath or alcohol sponging, which cause shivering and vasoconstriction; tepid sponging with lukewarm water only as an adjunct in a distressed child with a very high fever; fluids by mouth with an extra 10 to 12 per cent of maintenance for every degree above 37 °C; oral care and skin care for the sweating patient; rest; and observation — temperature, pulse, respiration, blood pressure, saturation, conscious level and urine output at a frequency the patient's condition sets, charted so that the trend is visible.",
